# T18252 — vlan classifier and tag vlan — tb470 IE520 stack — 2026-09-29 — PASS

Verdict: PASS. Both steps: every one of the five frame types (IPv4 192.168.10.x, IPv4 192.168.20.x,
IPX 0x8137, IPv6 0x86dd, "other" 0x88b5) was classified into the VLAN the rule names and egressed
exactly the ports the case predicts — proven by host captures behind two of the egress ports (100/100
of each set, with the right 802.1Q tag or none) and by per-port DUT output-packet deltas on all five
test ports (+100 on the predicted ports, +0 on the others). Run labelled: clean.

Operator: bench-runner subagent (device-testing-61 = sentinel, /test-mode shape A), Terrence away.
Queue: IE520/CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-29.md row 7 (VLAN group). Case text: ck.db AWPTCM-T18252 (2 steps).

## Bench mapping (case port -> DUT port -> observer)
The case uses vlan 10/20 and DUT port1.0.1-1.0.5 to an Ixia. On tb470 vlan 10 is the live sa1
transit to the AR4050S (LEFT ALONE), so the case VLANs became 210/220 (the rule set is otherwise
literal). Ports (group isolation recipe in README.md: sa2/sa3 legs freed into scratch VLANs 3991-3994):
| case          | role                                   | DUT port    | observed by |
| port1.0.1     | access + `vlan classifier activate 1`  | port3.0.13  | tb470 eth1 (direct; sender in step 1, capture in step 2) |
| port1.0.2     | trunk + `vlan classifier activate 1`   | port1.0.2   | tb470 eth3 via x230 port1.0.3->port1.0.1, both x230 ports `mode trunk`, allowed 210,220, native 3991 (tags preserved; sender in step 2) |
| port1.0.3     | access vlan 10 -> 210                  | port1.0.9   | DUT output counters (peer x230 port1.0.4 sits alone in vlan 3992) |
| port1.0.4     | access vlan 20 -> 220                  | port1.0.13  | tb470 eth2 via IE520-sa port1.0.13 (vlan 3993) -> SA port1.0.2 moved into 3993 for this case |
| port1.0.5     | trunk allowed 10,20 -> 210,220         | port4.0.9   | DUT output counters (peer SA port1.0.9 sits alone in vlan 3994) |
Extra bench port in vlan 1: port4.0.26 (the non-LAG SX link to the SA) — receives the vlan-1 flood
like any other vlan-1 member; counted and reported, not graded.

